---
title: Challah Bread
yield: 2%loaves
course: Bread
prep time: 30m
cook time: 25m
locale: en
difficulty: Medium
cuisine: Jewish
---

In a large mixing bowl dissolve the @fresh yeast{30%g} in 120 ml of the @warm water{300%ml}(hand-hot). Add the @caster sugar{50%g}, @vegetable oil{60%ml}, @salt{1%tsp}, and @egg{1}, and mix together well. Then add the remaining water.

Slowly stir in the @plain flour{600%g} and mix into a soft dough. Turn out onto a floured board and knead well for 5 minutes. Shape into a #very large bowl{} (with lots of room for expansion), cover with a #clean cloth{}, and leave in a warm place for ~{2%hours}.

Divide the dough into two balls. Put one aside and cover with a clean cloth until you are ready to work with it and divide the other into four smaller balls. Shape each ball into long strands. It is easiest to make the strands in stages - first taking each ball to 6", then 9", and then to the full 12" length.

To plait the four strands, pinch them all together at one end, then spread them out to form a double V-shape. Now thread the strands right-over-left for the pair on the right, and right-over-left for the pair on the left. Then for the pair in the middle take the left strand over the right one. Repeat this sequence until the end of the strands, then pinch them all together and tuck the join neatly under the end of the loaf.

Lightly beat the second @egg{1} with @water{a little} and glaze both loaves. Sprinkle @poppy seeds{} on top.

Place in a cold oven and then heat the oven to 160°C. Bake for ~{25%minutes}, checking from time to time. The loaves are done when they are nicely browned on top and give off a hollow sound when tapped on the bottom. Leave to cool on a wire rack.